  <main_text>
    <l n="1" para="true">De jure civili certum est, id</l>
    <l n="2">quod cuique debetur, aut</l>
    <l n="3">quod quisque sibi deberi exi-</l>
    <l n="4">stimat, non singularis certa-</l>
    <l n="5">minis eventu, sed actionibus</l>
    <l n="6">esse petendum; quæ ideo pro-</l>
    <l n="7">dita ut quisque jus suum per-</l>
    <l n="8">sequatur. Leges itaque prodi-</l>
    <l n="9">tæ, judices in civitate consti-</l>
    <l n="10">tuti, &amp; militares &amp; civiles, ut</l>
    <l n="11">militum civiumque inter se</l>
    <l n="12">controversias componant; &amp;</l>
    <l n="13">cuique quod suum est distri-</l>
    <l n="14">buant. Id equidem ita intel-</l>
    <l n="15">lexit Imperator Marcus An-</l>
    <l n="16">toninus, qui eam violentiæ</l>
    <l n="17">speciem judicavit, si quispiam</l>
    <l n="18">quod sibi deberi putat, sine ju-</l>
    <l n="19">dice poscat &amp; auferat. Ut in</l>
    <l n="20">judices injurii sint, qui sibimet</l>
    <l n="21">illam tribuunt potestatem, quæ</l>
    <l n="22">tantum erat judicum. Adeo-</l>
    <l n="23">que privati in publica munera</l>
    <l n="24">involent, distincta confundant</l>
    <l n="25">officia; &amp; ibi armis experian-</l>
    <l n="26">tur, ubi per legitimos judicio-</l>
    <l n="27">rum</l>
  </main_text>
